-- Arithmetic and `..` on a nullable must be a compile error. `check_operand_kind`
-- tests the *stripped* base type, so before this check an `integer?` passed the
-- "is it numeric?" test on the strength of the `integer` inside it, and the
-- failure surfaced at runtime instead.
--
-- Limited to identifiers, which is exactly the set `expr::narrow` can prove
-- non-nil — so `if count != nil then count + 1 end` still compiles.
class Main
	static fn main()
		local count: integer? = 1
		println(count + 1)
	end
end
